                "published_at": "Thu, 05 Nov 2026 15:15:15 GMT"
              },
              {
                "id": "i_bcbacce394a5",
                "title": "Ringside – Calculating the Extreme Cost of Floating Offshore Wind – California Globe",
                "url": "https://californiaglobe.com/fr/ringside-calculating-the-extreme-cost-of-floating-offshore-wind",
                "source": "californiaglobe.com",
                "summary": "None of these additional costs are going to go away simply because the source of the electricity is floating offshore wind. The energy supply portion of PG&E’s rate for electricity prior to these additional costs is around $0.11/kWh. While that price includes storage assets to buffer the intermittency of existing renewables, it doesn’t account for the expansion required to accommodate another 25 gigawatts of intermittent power. [...] Also necessary are upgrades to transmission lines, and that estimate can be found in the July 2024 “ISO 20-Year Transmission Outlook Update,” dated July 31, 2024. On page 68, table “Table ES-1: Cost estimate of transmission development to integrate resources of 2045 Scenario,” they estimate an additional cost of $36.5 billion for “transmission line installations, upgrades, substations, and grid interconnections for 25 GW.” [...] When it comes to the price of electricity in California, the financing cost is only the beginning. We have to add costs to operate and maintain these wind turbines. At the planned scale of 12 megawatts capacity per turbine, you are looking at maintenance for over 2,000 floating turbines, each of them approximately 800 feet tall",
